V R5 y | MainApp.tsx:1713-1715 now defines prWeight(p) = REPO_PRIORITY_WEIGHT[repo]+REPO_PRIORITY_WEIGHT[author] and groupWeight(g)=Math.max(...g.prs.map(prWeight)), used for both org/type/reason grouping sorts at lines 1725/1729.
  rebut This directly answers the quoted complaint: the code no longer does Math.min over repo-only weight (worst-repo-wins); it now takes Math.max (best PR in the group wins) over a per-PR weight that sums both repo AND author priority, so author priority is no longer dropped from group ranking. A group with one Critical-repo PR and one Standard-repo PR now gets groupWeight=Critical's weight, correctly outranking a group whose only repo is Important.
V R9 y | grep for '^<<<<<<<|^=======$|^>>>>>>>' across src/ and src-tauri/ returns zero matches; MainApp.tsx:2172 calls cycleNext(REPO_PRIORITY_ORDER, repoPrio ?? 'standard') and lines 2348-2349 call cycleNext(PR_PRIORITY_ORDER, menu.pr.priority), both imported from src/lib/priority.ts which defines REPO_PRIORITY_ORDER (6 entries) and PR_PRIORITY_ORDER (5 entries).
  rebut The prior verdict's blocker was unresolved merge conflict markers in MainApp.tsx making the cycle wiring unverifiable. A full marker scan of the tree now returns nothing, and the file compiles under tsc --noEmit with zero errors, so the cycle wiring (cycleNext over the single shared REPO_PRIORITY_ORDER/PR_PRIORITY_ORDER source of truth) is confirmed intact, not corrupted.
V R10 y | Read SettingsView.tsx:1230-1260 (Users/author table): one clean <select> with REPO_PRIORITY_ORDER.map producing <option> per level and a single 'Clear priority' button, no duplicated/corrupted JSX. RepoSettingsDrawer.tsx:101-106 and MainApp.tsx context menus (lines 2342-2419) also render all applicable levels via the same maps. grep found zero leftover 'normal'/'high'/'low' literals in these files.
  rebut The prior verdict's blocker was literal unresolved conflict markers duplicating/corrupting the author-table JSX at SettingsView.tsx:1298-1325 causing a TS1185 compile failure. That region no longer contains any conflict markers or duplicated JSX; tsc --noEmit now passes with zero errors, so the labels/options are directly readable and confirmed correct (six levels for repo/author, five for PR, all GTD-style labels, no 'normal'/'high'/'low' leftovers).
